Z||000017||RiceCooker||05-13-2000||08:17 PM||||Hey homicidal,<BR>I've got the same head unit and disc changer except my changer is mounted in the passenger side footwell and hidden quite well.<BR>How, you ask?<BR>well you can't hang it down from the air ducts obviously so I fastened a spray painted black piece of wood to the body and cut it so it fit around in behind the middle console. Then screwed the changer into the piece of wood. It's solid in there and i love it!<BR>Tell ya what, mail me your digital camera, and i'll post some pics for everyone!||||reg||6
Z||000018||olympic||05-14-2000||05:12 PM||||   Hey, I looked at a limo just like that on Tuesday in Winnipeg except it was a Buick Lesabre.  I had never seen a limo like it. It had the rear facing jump seats and was in pretty good shape.  I didn't buy it because the owner was doing a crappy job of restoring it and he wanted too much $$.<BR>  I didn't leave the city empty handed though.  I found an 84 Lesabre with only 60,000 miles on it!!  It was totally loaded with power moon roof and tinted windows. Beautiful showroom condition. I bought it on the spot.  It's almost a shame to use it as a taxi though but I take great care of my cars and she'll last me a long time.  People wonder why I buy these old Buicks for my fleet and I tell them its because I can buy 10 of them for the price of one new Grand Marquis and each of the Buicks will out last the Marquis and cost me a hell of a lot less in repairs.  I just retired an 82 Lesabre from my fleet.  She had over 300,000 hard miles and still ran like a champ on the origonal engine and trans.  I retired that car because it was rusting badly and looked bad. ||||reg||1
